{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791386829,"nonce":"jncL5xvnrhDMdCJl5CVnZutz54M=","title":"Closures in sibling source modules share lambda names","message":"MIR lambda names use the package name plus a per-lowering counter. Two files in package unit can both generate \u003clambda_unit__0\u003e. Same-named closure targets and their generated entries can consequently resolve to a sibling implementation with a different return representation or environment layout.\n\nThe native-verified sibling_closures regression project imports a source module with capturing Int-\u003eString callbacks and declares an Int identity closure in the entry module. On b08840ff62933d296bdf70adf9213605bf5902dc, native Haxe prints CONFORMANCE_OK; pinned Cranelift segfaults at null in \u003clambda_unit__0\u003e while calling the entry module's identity closure. A single-module control and an imported module without capturing callbacks both pass. Baseline output is /tmp/rayzor-sibling-closures-before-v1.log.\n\nTestMisc also contains \u003clambda_unit__1\u003e in both unit/MyClass.hx (Int-\u003eString) and unit/TestMisc.hx (Int-\u003eInt), with different captured environments. MIR and CLIF dumps are /tmp/rayzor-testmisc-mir-baseline.log and /tmp/rayzor-testmisc-bind-clif.log. This is a concrete shared cause to investigate for the bound-method wrong answers and dynamic closure crash; the full corpus will establish the affected score.","files":null}]}